# Taint Command and State File Management:
› State file is very important. Because inside state file, the current information about our VM is present. If suppose any machine is having problem and we want to delete it and recreate it back. Then if we hit terraform apply it will not recreate the machine. It will simply show 0 added, 0 destroyed, 0 changed.
› So instead of deleting state file, because deleting state file can bring lots of overhead and lots of problem.

# We use taint command as:
› terraform taint <resource_type>.<alias_name>
› Eg -> terraform taint aws_instance.linux_vm
*(Note: linux_vm is the alias name which we used in our script)
› By hitting the above command, we are marking that resource as a corrupted/bad resource inside the state file, indicating that it needs to be recreated.

› If we change our mind and do not want to recreate the resource, we can untaint it:
› terraform untaint aws_instance.linux_vm

# Taint Lifecycle and Practical Observations:
› Suppose we have a script to create an EC2 VM name -> Server-X.
› When we hit taint command:
› aws_instance.linux_vm has been marked as tainted.
› Next when we execute -> sudo terraform apply --auto-approve (while Server-X is already running).
› Then first it will destroy the resource, and then it will re-create the resource with the same name Server-X.
*(Note: We can change the name after taint to get better clarity on AWS web console)
*During practice, the old machine "Server-New" got deleted and "Server-New-Tainted" got created. (New machine got created).

# Advanced Taint Behaviors and Introduction to Terraform Modules:
› Again and Again if we have to taint we have to get our resource to tainted state
› And if we want to get a tainted resource to normal state we use the untaint command as -
› sudo terraform untaint aws_instance.linux_vm

*Now the thing is to we taint we again and again have to use the taint command to get to tainted state
› eg. if i changed the name in main.tf and didn't went to tainted mode, I noticed that the source got changed rather than being recreated after deletion.
› If we try to untaint an untainted resource we get a warning only.
